Ouagadougou, November 25, 2025 - The Technical Committee on Social Mutualism (COTEMS) is holding its fourth ordinary session of the year from November 25 to 27, 2025, in Ouagadougou.

Organized by the Ministry of Public Service, Labor, and Social Protection (MFPTPS) through the Directorate General of Social Protection (DGPS), this session represents the final review of 2025, focusing on the accreditation and registration of social mutual organizations.

According to Amédée Bamouni, President of COTEMS, the session will examine key applications, including:

The accreditation application of the Mutual Society of Workers of the Rural Electrification Agency of Burkina Faso (MUTA_ABER);

The registration file of the Federation of Community Mutual Social Societies (FEMUSCO), an umbrella organization;

The accreditation application of the National Police Mutual Society (MU_POL);

And any other matters within the Committee’s mandate.

COTEMS is responsible for processing applications for legal recognition of mutual societies and umbrella organizations, as well as monitoring and auditing accredited entities to ensure compliance with national regulations.

From July 2023 to October 2025, the Committee processed numerous applications, culminating in the approval and registration of over one hundred mutual societies, including federations or groups of mutuals that successfully met the required criteria.

This session underscores the Burkinabe government’s commitment to strengthening social protection, promoting collective solidarity, and ensuring that mutual societies operate under legal and regulatory frameworks that protect their members.

body_fr:
Ouagadougou, le 25 novembre 2025 - Le Comité Technique sur le Mutualisme Social (COTEMS) tient sa quatrième session ordinaire de l'année du 25 au 27 novembre 2025, à Ouagadougou.

Organisée par le Ministère de la Fonction Publique, du Travail et de la Protection Sociale (MFPTPS) à travers la Direction Générale de la Protection Sociale (DGPS), cette session représente la dernière révision de 2025, axée sur l'accréditation et l'enregistrement des organisations mutualistes sociales.

Selon Amédée Bamouni, Président du COTEMS, la session examinera des demandes clés, notamment :

La demande d'accréditation de la Mutuelle des Travailleurs de l'Agence Burkinabè d'Électrification Rurale (MUTA_ABER) ;

Le dossier d'enregistrement de la Fédération des Mutuelles Sociales Communautaires (FEMUSCO), une organisation faîtière ;

La demande d'accréditation de la Mutuelle de la Police Nationale (MU_POL) ;

Et toute autre question relevant du mandat du Comité.

Le COTEMS est chargé de traiter les demandes de reconnaissance légale des sociétés mutualistes et des organisations faîtières, ainsi que de surveiller et d'auditer les entités accréditées pour garantir leur conformité aux réglementations nationales.

De juillet 2023 à octobre 2025, le Comité a traité de nombreuses demandes, aboutissant à l'approbation et à l'enregistrement de plus d'une centaine de sociétés mutualistes, y compris des fédérations ou des groupes de mutualistes ayant satisfait aux critères requis.

Cette session souligne l'engagement du gouvernement burkinabè à renforcer la protection sociale, à promouvoir la solidarité collective et à garantir que les sociétés mutualistes opèrent dans des cadres légaux et réglementaires qui protègent leurs membres.

body_en:
Ouagadougou, November 24, 2025 - The Minister of Public Service, Labor, and Social Protection, Mathias Traoré, presented on Monday the performance report for the 2025 budget and unveiled the strategic orientations for 2026 before the Finance and Budget Committee (COMFIB) of the Transitional Legislative Assembly (ALT). This hearing forms part of the annual budget evaluation process for government departments.

From the outset, the Minister expressed appreciation for the ALT’s continued collaboration, before providing a detailed overview of the ministry’s four priority programs:

Human Resources Management (Program 042), which aims to modernize personnel administration and improve career monitoring;

Administrative Reform (Program 043), focused on enhancing public sector efficiency and digitizing procedures;

Promotion of Decent Work (Program 044), dedicated to reinforcing labor standards, strengthening social dialogue, and combating precarious employment;

Management of Departmental Services (Program 045), which supports decentralized administrative operations.

The exchanges with COMFIB members were vigorous and substantial, covering several structural challenges currently facing the public administration. Deputies questioned the Minister on key issues such as:

the implementation of Universal Health Insurance (RAMU);

the new targeted recruitment model, designed to better align hires with priority needs;

the fight against fraudulent diplomas;

mandatory medical examinations for retirees;

the situation of laid-off workers and compensation mechanisms;

the enforcement of the law on political neutrality within the civil service;

and the current state of the public workforce, including staffing levels and distribution.

Minister Traoré assured the committee that actions have already been initiated across all these areas, noting that “efforts will be intensified in 2026 to accelerate implementation and address remaining gaps.”

Looking toward the upcoming year, he reaffirmed the government’s determination to deepen ongoing reforms in the civil service, strengthen labor governance mechanisms, and expand social protection coverage. These priorities, he said, remain guided by a central objective: protecting the rights, working conditions, and general interests of all workers.

The Minister also appealed for the ALT’s sustained support, especially to complete the deployment of the RAMU, whose operational benefits are expected to begin on February 1, 2026. He stressed that this reform represents “a major social turning point” for improving access to healthcare for the population.

